# UpSnap Provider

The UpSnap provider manages an [UpSnap](https://github.com/seriousm4x/UpSnap) instance through its PocketBase API.
It is tested against UpSnap 5.7.3.

## Example Usage

{{ tffile "examples/provider/provider.tf" }}

## Authentication

UpSnap is a PocketBase application and accepts two kinds of accounts:

* **Superuser** (default, `auth_collection = "_superusers"`): the account created during UpSnap's setup wizard or with
  `upsnap superuser upsert <email> <password>`. Required for `upsnap_port`, `upsnap_user`, `upsnap_permission` and `upsnap_settings`.
* **UpSnap user** (`auth_collection = "users"`): can manage the devices and groups its `upsnap_permission` allows, and run power actions on
  devices it has `power` permission for.

Instead of a password you can pass a PocketBase `token`, for example a superuser impersonation token
(PocketBase dashboard → `_superusers` → record → *Impersonate*). Refreshable tokens are refreshed before they expire; with
`username` and `password` the provider logs in again whenever needed. Credentials are verified when the provider is configured.

| Argument               | Environment variable          |
| ---------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| `endpoint`             | `UPSNAP_ENDPOINT`             |
| `username`             | `UPSNAP_USERNAME`             |
| `password`             | `UPSNAP_PASSWORD`             |
| `token`                | `UPSNAP_TOKEN`                |
| `auth_collection`      | `UPSNAP_AUTH_COLLECTION`      |
| `insecure_skip_verify` | `UPSNAP_INSECURE_SKIP_VERIFY` |
| `settle_time`          | `UPSNAP_SETTLE_TIME`          |

A `token` set in the configuration wins over everything else; otherwise `username` and `password` win over a token from the environment.

## Importing existing objects

Every resource can be imported with `terraform import`, an `import` block with `id`, or (Terraform 1.12+) an `import` block with
`identity`. Records are imported by their PocketBase record ID, shown in the PocketBase dashboard at `<endpoint>/_/`.
Users can also be imported by `username:<name>`, permissions by `user:<user id>` or `username:<name>`, and the settings with the ID `settings`.
Devices and groups have no unique natural key in UpSnap, so they are imported by ID only.

## Power actions

Waking, shutting down, suspending and rebooting are imperative, so they are [actions](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/invoke-actions)
(Terraform 1.14+) rather than resources: trigger them from a resource's `action_trigger`, or run them with
`terraform apply -invoke=action.upsnap_device_wake.<name>`.

## Known issues

### UpSnap can revert device and port changes

UpSnap 5.7.3's ping job loads every device, pings it, and, when the status changed, saves the copy it loaded before the ping.
Any change made in between (to a device's fields, groups or ports) is silently reverted; ports are saved the same way after
their port check.

The provider works around this: after it changes a device, links or moves a port, or changes a port, it watches the record
for `settle_time` (5 seconds by default) and writes the change again as soon as UpSnap reverts it, up to three times. If UpSnap
keeps reverting it, the apply fails with an error that lists the reverted fields.

The default covers UpSnap's built-in ping, which takes at most about 2.5 seconds. Raise `settle_time` if the error shows up,
which can happen when:

* a device has a slow custom `ping_cmd` (UpSnap gives it no timeout),
* a device has many ports (each port check takes up to half a second), or
* a wake, shutdown, sleep or reboot is still running for the device (UpSnap saves the device when it finishes, and a reboot
  waits 15 seconds).

Set `settle_time = "0s"` to turn the check off, for example when UpSnap's `lazy_ping` is on and nobody has the dashboard open,
which stops the ping job.

